{#
  /order-online/. Brief: SITE_ARCHITECTURE.md.

  Third-biggest page on the site: 5,648 clicks, 355,269 impressions.
  Transactional intent. The brief's whole section plan is four words long:
  "One card per location. Name, neighborhood, Toast link. Nothing else. This
  page's only job is not to be in the way." So there is no hero video, no
  story, no noodle band. Cards and out.

  ORDERING URLS: VERIFIED 2026-08-05 by click-test, all seven. This was the
  biggest open risk on the page and it is closed. All three problems the brief
  flagged are resolved:

    - The two competing URL patterns (/v2/online-order versus /v3) are settled
      on one, order.toasttab.com/online/<slug>, across all seven.
    - Crown Point's slug still reads `tajima-pacific-beach-3782-ingraham-street`
      and is deliberately kept. The destination is Crown Point; the slug is
      stale Toast routing, and editing it breaks the cart.
    - Plaza Bonita's `toasttakeout.page.link` shortlink is retired for a real
      toasttab URL. Its slug says suite 2075 while the real suite is 2445;
      we publish 2445 and link 2075, same reasoning as Crown Point.

  Maui now has a URL too, so every card renders an order button. The `if
  loc.orderUrl` guard below stays as a guard, not because a location is
  missing one.

  The Locations mega menu now links Toast directly rather than routing through
  this page. Both surfaces read loc.orderUrl, so they cannot drift.
#}

<section class="order-hero" aria-labelledby="page-title">
  <div class="order-hero__inner">
    <h1 class="order-hero__title" id="page-title">Order Online</h1>
    <p class="order-hero__sub">
      Pick the room you want it from. Pickup and delivery, straight through to that kitchen's order page. <a href="/menu/">See the menu</a> first if you want.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="order-grid" aria-label="Order by location">
  <div class="order-grid__inner">
    {%- for loc in locations.items %}
    {#
      The id was the anchor target for /order-online/#<slug>, which is where
      the mega menu's order buttons used to point. As of 2026-08-05 they link
      Toast directly, so nothing on the site depends on these anchors any more.
      They are kept because the URLs are live: /order-online/#convoy may exist
      in a bookmark, a GBP post, or an old email, and an anchor that silently
      stops resolving is a broken link nobody reports.
    #}
    <article class="order-card" id="{{ loc.id }}">
      <div>
        <h2 class="order-card__name">{{ loc.name }}</h2>
        {#- Dedupe: Plaza Bonita and Maui have neighborhood == locality, which
            printed "National City, National City". Same guard the home router uses. -#}
        <p class="order-card__hood">
          {%- if loc.neighborhood and loc.neighborhood != loc.address.locality -%}
            {{ loc.neighborhood }}, {{ loc.address.locality }}
          {%- else -%}
            {{ loc.address.locality }}, {{ loc.address.region }}
          {%- endif -%}
        </p>
      </div>

      {%- if loc.orderUrl %}
      <a class="order-card__cta" href="{{ loc.orderUrl }}" rel="noopener">
        Order from {{ loc.name }}
        <span aria-hidden="true">&#8594;</span>
      </a>
      {%- else %}
      {# No ordering URL on record. Offer the phone, which is confirmed, rather
         than a button that goes nowhere. #}
      <a class="order-card__cta order-card__cta--quiet" href="tel:+1-{{ loc.phone | replace('(', '') | replace(') ', '-') | replace(' ', '-') }}">
        Call {{ loc.phone }}
      </a>
      {%- endif %}
    </article>
    {%- endfor %}
  </div>
</section>

<section class="loc-close" aria-labelledby="close-title">
  <div class="loc-close__inner">
    <h2 class="loc-close__title" id="close-title">Not sure which room?</h2>
    <p class="loc-close__sub">
      <a href="/locations/">All six San Diego rooms</a>, with addresses and phone numbers.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
